1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,600
Your coding agents are capable.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,100
But every session starts from zero.

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:07,500
They re-read the same files.

4
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:10,000
They retry the fix your team reverted.

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,400
After compaction, they lose their place.

6
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,900
And you pay for every re-read in tokens.

7
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,300
Cairn is institutional memory for coding agents.

8
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,000
Five layers: map, specs, timeline, memory and sessions.

9
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,600
Before an edit, an agent asks Cairn: impact, why, context.

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,800
It gets a cited answer, inside a token budget.

11
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,800
On Cairn's own repo, one answer took 391 tokens,

12
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:37,600
instead of 144,228 tokens across 33 files.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,600
After compaction, the next session picks up where it left off.

14
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,800
One command in any git repo. Local. No API key.

15
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,200
Works with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini, Copilot and OpenCode.

16
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:56,000
Cairn. Memory your agents can cite.
